s point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317394 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "086a93d6-9edf-5e6c-82a6-6ae6a865d444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317394 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375035 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375035 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375035 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375035 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375035 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "73e9342f-7d4b-572d-ae06-c85b2c42d4ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375036 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb5a35bc-6e98-5466-b248-220b58805210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf7482ba-c42e-5af4-af91-a8b902345ce3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375039 #edb/ref 1080 "(-1)^{n+1}" #ed